  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\nIssued at 856 AM CDT Sat Jul 18 2026\n\nThe Mid-South will shift into a northwest flow regime this\nweekend as a subtropical ridge builds over the western Gulf and a\ntropical disturbance emerges over the Florida peninsula.\nWeaknesses within the aforementioned ridge will keep rain chances\nin the forecast both Saturday and Sunday. The greatest chance for\nprecipitation will exist in areas near the Tennessee River.\nAfternoon highs will be in the low to mid 90s. The NBM continues\nto paint areas of 105 degree heat indices within the Mid-South\nboth Saturday and Sunday. However, nearly all ensemble guidance\nkeeps apparent temperatures below this threshold, negating the\nneed for a Heat Advisory. There are no plans at this time for any\nheat headlines this weekend, but we will continue to evaluate as\nmore data becomes available.\n\nEarly next week will experience significant warming as the\nsubtropical ridge centers over the Mid-South. GEFS, GEPS, and ENS\nsolutions are in agreement with temperatures reaching the upper\n90s by Tuesday afternoon. At this time, there is high confidence\nthat heat headlines will be needed Monday and Tuesday with the\npotential for Extreme Heat Warnings along and west of the\nMississippi River. Our advice for those that must be outside\nduring this time is to hydrate frequently and take plenty of\nbreaks. Heat remains the number one cause of weather-related\nfatalities in the United States, so stay informed and learn the\nsigns of heat illness.\n\nBy midweek, guidance continues to bring a cold front into the Mid-\nSouth. However, forecast uncertainty remains due to ensembles\nstruggling with the precise placement of the parent low pressure\nsystem. If this disturbance treks more northerly, the Mid-South\nwill experience minimal relief with highs only decreasing to the\nmid 90s through the end of the work week. The cooler option will\nmanifest if the upper low treks over Lakes Erie and Ontario. This\nwill produce highs in the upper 80s to low 90s. Due to these\ninconsistencies, the NBM continues to exhibit a 7 degree spread\namongst the 25th and 75th percentiles for the middle to end of\nnext week. Regardless of which solution occurs, temperatures will\nremain warm with minimal rain chances.\n\nANS",
